# ── Consolidated posts — 301 to the landing page that now owns the keyword ──
#
# These two posts competed head-on with their own landing pages for the same
# commercial-intent queries, splitting authority three ways. Both were removed
# from blogData.ts, which also drops them from the generated sitemap and the
# prerender list. They must sit ABOVE the trailing-slash rule below, or that rule
# claims them first and they end up at /blog/ via the client-side redirect.
# Both the bare and trailing-slash forms are listed because either can be linked.
/blog/snaprec-vs-loom-free-alternative   /loom-alternative/           301
/blog/snaprec-vs-loom-free-alternative/  /loom-alternative/           301
/blog/screencastify-vs-snaprec-free-alternative   /screencastify-alternative/  301
/blog/screencastify-vs-snaprec-free-alternative/  /screencastify-alternative/  301

# Trailing slash — explicit 301 (overrides Cloudflare Pages' default 308)
# Must stay above the SPA rewrites below, or /blog gets rewritten instead of redirected.
/blog/:slug  /blog/:slug/  301
/about/ghulam-muhammad  /about/ghulam-muhammad/  301
/blog  /blog/  301
/about  /about/  301
/how-it-works  /how-it-works/  301
/changelog  /changelog/  301
/privacy  /privacy/  301
/terms  /terms/  301
/contact  /contact/  301
/loom-alternative  /loom-alternative/  301
/screencastify-alternative  /screencastify-alternative/  301
/webcam-overlay-presentation  /webcam-overlay-presentation/  301
/screen-recorder-for-teachers  /screen-recorder-for-teachers/  301

# The uninstall survey needs its 301 here, in the block that runs BEFORE the
# rewrites, for the same reason every route above does. Without it Cloudflare's
# own trailing-slash normalisation claimed the bare path first and 308'd it to
# `/` — so chrome.runtime.setUninstallURL, which points at the bare path, sent
# every uninstalling user to the landing page and the survey was never shown.
# Extensions already in the wild have the bare URL stored in the browser and
# will keep requesting it forever, so this line is what fixes them.
/uninstall-survey  /uninstall-survey/  301

# Redirect pages.dev traffic to production
https://snaprecorder.pages.dev/*  https://www.snaprecorder.org/:splat  301

# ── Known routes get the SPA shell; everything else gets a real 404 ────────
#
# The original config was a single `/*  /index.html  200`, which gave every
# typo, stale link and crawler probe HTTP 200 with an empty body — an unbounded
# soft-404 surface. Enumerating the real routes here is what allows the rest to
# 404 without taking the client-only routes (the signed-in app, the editors and
# every /v/ share link) down with them; none of those have a file on disk, and
# Slack, X and LinkedIn refuse to unfurl a 404.
#
# The 404 itself comes from dist/404.html, not from a rule in this file — see
# the note at the bottom. Every route here matches one in src/App.tsx, and
# src/__tests__/routes.test.ts fails if one is added there without a rule here.

# Public marketing routes, served from their prerendered files on disk.
#
# These lines are NOT a working fallback for a build where prerender did not
# run: they are exact-path 200 proxies, which Pages does not honour (the app
# block below explains). They are kept because they document the route list and
# cost nothing, but if prerender stops running these pages 404 — the build is
# the thing that guarantees they exist, not this file.
/  /index.html  200
/how-it-works/  /index.html  200
/changelog/  /index.html  200
/privacy/  /index.html  200
/terms/  /index.html  200
/about/  /index.html  200
/about/ghulam-muhammad/  /index.html  200
/contact/  /index.html  200
/blog/  /index.html  200
/blog/*  /index.html  200
/loom-alternative/  /index.html  200
/screencastify-alternative/  /index.html  200
/webcam-overlay-presentation/  /index.html  200
/screen-recorder-for-teachers/  /index.html  200

# Auth, the signed-in app, the editors and share links. Never prerendered, all
# noindex or robots-disallowed, so a 200 here costs nothing in search.
#
# EVERY RULE IN THIS BLOCK ENDS IN `*`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
#
# Cloudflare Pages does not honour an exact-path 200 proxy to /index.html. The
# previous version of this block spelled these out as `/editor  /index.html
# 200`, and every one of them answered 308 -> / in production while the file
# looked completely correct: /editor, /login, /home, /library, /projects,
# /shared, /analytics, /settings, /claim, /dashboard and /video-editor all
# landed on the marketing page instead of the app. The wildcard forms
# (/editor/*, /auth/*, /v/*) sitting beside them worked the whole time, which
# is what makes the pattern visible.
#
# A trailing `*` also covers the bare path, so one rule per route replaces the
# pair, and /editor, /editor/ and /editor/:id all resolve.
/login*  /index.html  200
/auth/*  /index.html  200
/claim*  /index.html  200
/dashboard*  /index.html  200
/home*  /index.html  200
/library*  /index.html  200
/projects*  /index.html  200
/shared*  /index.html  200
/analytics*  /index.html  200
/settings*  /index.html  200
/editor*  /index.html  200
# Both /video-* rules must precede /v*, which is a prefix of them and would
# otherwise claim them. Same destination either way today, but the ordering is
# what keeps that true if these ever diverge. /v* rather than /v/* so the bare
# /v resolves too — the route is /v/:id?, so the id is optional.
/video-editor*  /index.html  200
/video-preview*  /index.html  200
/v*  /index.html  200

# The uninstall survey. Unlinked and unindexed, but it must still resolve — it is
# the page the browser opens when SnapRec is removed, so a 404 here would greet
# every uninstalling user at the exact moment we are asking for feedback.
# The bare path 301s to the slashed form up in the block above; this serves it.
/uninstall-survey*  /index.html  200

# No catch-all. Anything reaching this point matches no asset and no rule, and
# Pages answers it with the top-level 404.html that vite.config.ts emits.
#
# There used to be a `/*  /index.html  404` here, which never worked: Pages
# supports redirects and 200 proxies in this file but not 404 rewrites, so the
# status was quietly downgraded and every typo, stale link and crawler probe
# still got HTTP 200 — the exact soft-404 surface that rule was written to
# remove. Worse, its presence meant the project had no top-level 404.html, and
# without one Pages assumes a single-page app and redirects every unmatched
# route to `/`. That assumption is what sent the bare app routes above to the
# homepage. Deleting the rule and shipping a real 404.html fixes both.
/*  /index.html  200
